.TH HORSEPOWER 1 "November 2026" "HorsePower 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
horsepower \- see how much electricity this computer is using
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B horsepower
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B horsepower
opens a small window that shows the power the machine is drawing right now,
a breakdown of where that power goes, the energy it has used today, the energy
it has used since HorsePower was installed, and a chart of the last hour.
.PP
The breakdown lists the CPU package, the graphics card, memory and storage, and
whatever remains of the platform total. A component appears only when the
machine can genuinely measure it; unmeasurable parts are omitted rather than
shown as zero. When the firmware exposes a platform-wide counter the caption
reads
.IR "system power" ,
otherwise it reads
.IR "measured parts" .
.PP
The readings come from the energy counters that Intel and AMD processors
expose through
.IR /sys/class/powercap ,
from NVML or hwmon for graphics cards, and from the battery's power attribute
on machines without any of those.
.PP
Samples are collected by
.BR horsepowerd (8),
which runs in the background, so totals continue to accumulate while the
window is closed.
.PP
The cost shown in the window uses the tariff entered in
.IR Preferences .
Both the tariff and the log stay on this computer; nothing is uploaded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/horsepower/settings.json
The electricity tariff and currency used for cost estimates.
.TP
.I /var/lib/horsepower/power.db
The SQLite log written by the background service.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HORSEPOWER_BUS
Set to
.I session
to talk to a user session bus instead of the system bus. Intended for
development.
.TP
.B HORSEPOWER_DEBUG
Enable verbose application logging.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR horsepowerd (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
.SH BUGS
Fans are not individually metered by any mainstream Linux interface, so they
only appear inside "rest of system" when a platform counter exists. Displays
and USB peripherals are outside the platform counter on most desktops, so on a
machine without a platform total the number describes the computer's brain,
not the whole desk.
